Now as it ever was:  the respectful and right thing for the
BuglistingTeam to do is to relinquish their claim on official NetHack
maintainership, which includes releasing any bugfix code to the net so
that work doesn't have to be endlessly repeated by each coder
haplessly trying to put together a branch, giving up the domain name,
and in general coming to grips with their dormancy, making a public
announcement and passing the reins on to the collective will of the
net, where some other group with more vitality and interest will give
the game new life.  The "devteam" we have now has been dead weight on
the game for years, causing prolonged and unnecessary waiting, much
unnecessary bifurcation and speculation, and work wasted by
duplication of effort many times over.  The 2 or 3 major variants
produced in the last 8 years seem like good efforts, nothing wrong
with them, but development would be more unified and focused and
dynamic if everybody wasn't pussyfooting around a dead "devteam" and
their never-forthcoming next move.

Main idea for the "devteam" to take from the above paragraph:  know
when to pass the reins.  It's part of the dignity of aging.

EIGHT years, people.

